The page is a short working note about the naive (algebraic) de Rham complex of a singular or non-reduced k-algebra, with k of characteristic 0. 'Lemme de Poincaré vrai' means that the complex Ω^•_{A/k} (completed in the formal cases) is a resolution of k: H^0 = k and H^i = 0 for i > 0.

The examples listed are:
(1) the cusp-type formal curves k[[x]][Y]/(Y^p − x^q);
(2) a general double cover k[[x]][Y]/(Y^2+P(x)Y+Q(x)). In characteristic 0 one completes the square to get Y'^2 = R(x) = x^m·unit, and after taking a root of the unit this is Y^2 = x^m, a case of (1);
(3) truncated polynomial algebras k[x_1..x_n]/(x_i^{p_i}). For k[x]/(x^p) one checks directly that Ω = ⊕_{j≤p−2} k x^j dx and d: x^i ↦ i x^{i−1}dx is onto with kernel k. The general case follows by Künneth;
(4) k[x,y]/m^2. Here dim A = 3, Ω^1 = ⟨dx, dy, x dy⟩ with x dy = −y dx, and Ω^2 = k dx∧dy, so the complex 3 → 3 → 1 is exact except for H^0 = k;
(5) k[x,y]/(x^3, y^2, x^2y) and k[x,y]/(x^3, y^2, xy).

Every example is quasi-homogeneous: either weighted-homogeneous in suitable weights, or defined by monomial ideals. The common mechanism is the Euler derivation E. The Cartan formula L_E = d i_E + i_E d acts on a form of weight w by multiplication by w. That is invertible in characteristic 0 for w > 0, so positive-weight cohomology vanishes.

The lower block reads, roughly: 'If A is local over k (the word 'artinien' is crossed out and replaced by 'local'), with trivial residue extension, then for x ∈ m with dx = 0 one has x ∈ m^3.' This is a first step toward H^0 = k for local algebras. First, dx = 0 implies x ∈ m^2, because Ω_A ⊗ k = m/m^2. Next, write A = P/I with I ⊂ m_P^2 and W = m/m^2. Then mΩ_A/m^2Ω_A = (W⊗W)/Q, where Q is the image of the quadratic parts of I. Also m^2/m^3 = Sym^2 W/Q. Because Sym^2 W → W⊗W is injective in characteristic 0, dx ≡ 0 forces x ∈ m^3.

The last lines are a bibliographic memo: 'Ann. Éc. N. Sc. [65 ou 66] ou J. de Liouville', with the identification uncertain. This fits Grothendieck's 1965–69 program of replacing the naive de Rham complex by crystalline/infinitesimal cohomology precisely because the naive Poincaré lemma fails for general singularities.
